  "body": "Exact maximum of leaf-destination transport: max(1, floor(log2 n))\n\n## Contribution\n\nNew registered package `research/leaf-destination-transport-bound`.\n\n**Theorem.** In PR628's balanced reserved-depth dirty word with n free, noncommuting, contractive factors (both orientations), a unit error injected at the destination of a completed leaf has, at every later cut and in every register, free-algebra mass at most max(1, floor(log2 n)). An explicit family attains this for every n >= 2. So **E(n) = max(1, floor(log2 n)) for all n**.\n\n- **Upper bound (Theorem 1):** Lemmas S/L2 (prefix entries are 0 or one signed monomial, and the destination\u2013source entry is 0 or s\u00b7P_K), L1 (exit column), I (the sign \u2212 call is the leaf-by-leaf inverse) and X (an exit column has at most floor(log2 k)+1 nonzero registers, at most floor(log2 k) of them scratch), then a case table over the lowest common call.\n- **Attainment (Theorem 2):** the injection follows the y-keeping chain U, U', U, \u2026 and the readout is at the V-chain cut (Lemma P). The closed form has H = floor(log2 n) terms with distinct lengths, so none of them cancel.\n\nThis answers two open questions recorded on main:\n- `research/leaf-destination-transport`: \"Does `max(1,floor(log2 n))` hold for every n?\" Yes.\n- PR1416 `research/single-register-interval-transport`: \"Determine the exact maximum between h\u22121 and h+2.\" For destination-supported single errors the maximum is floor(log2 n). That equals h when n is a power of two and h\u22121 otherwise.\n\nNo kappa, precision-invoice or rounding claim is made. The package does not cover multiple or accumulated errors, internal leaf values, rounding or aliasing.\n\n**Not independently reviewed.** The proof is written out in `PROOF.md`. Exact finite checks support it, but no one other than the author has reviewed it, and there is no formal (Lean) proof.\n\n## Provenance\n\n- Card `uniform-transfer-audit`, track adversarial-audit, priority revision 4, chooser nonce `9d450e657d814dfba70c4f6537e46fac`, scope key `ce7411fb7cf6f3460bfc`. Scope is `refined` (see `TASK.json` and `TASK-CARD.md`).\n- Base `0fdd7d82de03e43c9e5a1000dfbcd380d1b687d2`. The pinned live input is `research/dirty-cubic-precision/vendor/628/dirty.py`. The verifier checks that the word generators match its literal `compose` for n <= 40.\n- The card's first step (`scripts/research_baseline.py`) was skipped during the research session because a disk quota cut an extraction short. It was run at packaging time and printed `PASS_RESEARCH_BASELINE_BINDINGS_ONLY`.\n- Predecessors: woklerx (`leaf-destination-transport`), cbethin (PR1416), Arham Tahir (PR1374), Hanson Sun (PR1085), Chafik Boukhalfa (PR628). Adjacent open PR: #2067 (endpoint accumulation).\n\n## Reproduce\n\n```sh\npython3 scripts/research_package.py --package research/leaf-destination-transport-bound --check-only\npython3.12 scripts/research_package.py --package research/leaf-destination-transport-bound\n```\n\n`verify.py` uses only the standard library and takes about 20 s. It runs:\n- `check2` for n = 1..40, covering Lemma I, L1, Lemma X, the case table, E(n) and Lemma P;\n- the attaining family up to n = 128;\n- the family closed form for n = 2..200;\n- Lemmas L1, L2 and S for n <= 32;\n- the independent census for n <= 24;\n- two mutation controls that must be rejected. The unreversed inverse gives E(7) = 54, and the ceil-half split gives E(6) = 3.\n\nLonger runs by the author are in `logs/`: n = 1..80, the family up to n = 512, and the census for n = 71..128.\n\n## AI assistance\n\nPrepared by Philippe Van Velzen with substantial Claude (Anthropic) assistance. Claude sub-agents wrote the proof, the census and the checking scripts, and the main session re-ran the checks and controls.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
